 one  error that was preventing it from  running I read I searched  question whether I should just give up  throw in the towel and go back to my old  job making pretty stock price charts CS  107 that intermediate computer science  class and with a big assignment it's  called the heath allocator the heap both  stores and retrieves data the class  assignments evaluated both on accuracy  and time benchmarks I was convinced that  I was going to build the best heap that  I could I wanted to prove to myself I  belonged in that class so I set out over  Thanksgiving break to optimize my heap  keep coding keep trying and I couldn't  have been more pleased by the results if  I were to compare my heap to an Olympic  hurdler it not only would have been in  the final heap but it would have run a  bright shining medal it ran through the  assign task without making a single  mistake I couldn't have been prouder all  of those late nights were paying off I  was becoming a true coder a similar  victory is available to you you can make  it through the ups and downs and achieve  an incredibly worthy goal becoming a  coder I've talked to a lot of us with a  lot of my friends at Stanford about  learning how to code through those  conversations I've identified three  common questions the first common  question is what really is coding coding  is how humans boss computers are wrapped  we often talk about writing code but I  like to think about is collecting code  there's so many great programs online  today these can be combined together to  create something that's bigger than the  sum of its parts  what that means for you is that you can  get a lot of mileage out of the basic  understanding of coding you can stand on  the shoulders of giants and with a basic  understanding use the latest machine  learning algorithms or impress your  friends whatever else you want to make  up the second question often asked is  why is this important  technology has had the power to  continuously improve our lives yet our  skill set has needed to keep up to be  able to take advantage of those advances  for example think about typing like on a  typewriter in the 1950s only specialist  secretaries typed they sat in the middle  of office and processed everyone's  documents but time passed and in the  1980s personal computers became  prevalent then it was no longer  sufficient for just secretaries to type  everyone in the office needed to learn  how that's brought us today where typing  is an omnipresent skill  I expect we'll go through the same  trajectory with coding we're already  past the point where only engineers are  specialists in this case code out of the  top quartile jobs by ranked by salary  50% already requires some sort of coding  or technical skills so that means that  there are the marketers data scientists  these ops people those beyond engineers  who are already coding to me it's  inevitable that this trend continues  that we'll reach a point where all  people in all roles are coding you may  be thinking to yourself at this point  I'm going to be a manager I'll have my  engineers to do all the coding what a  good wishful thinking to be able to  effectively communicate with engineers  an understanding of code is critical it  helps you speak the same language and be  able to do your work faster and more  effectively  the third question I'm often asked is  how do I get started we are incredibly  fortunate Stanford to have incredible  coding resources available to us it's as  if we've stumbled into Sam Stanford  Sistine Chapel of computer science we  can look up and learn a ton from  Michelangelo stealing CS 106a one of the  most popular students one of the most  popular classes for MBA students  is being offered this spring of course  there are online resources too  one of my favorites is Cote Academy's  Python class in under 10 hours you can  go from knowing nothing about Python to  having a solid understanding of the  language and being able to build your  own programs you may be thinking at this  point when am I going to make time to  code I can barely do my laundry as is me  - ah  I completely understand we have so many  special opportunities available to us  here however if you're at all interested  in coding now is the best time to learn  we have access to unparalleled resources  at Stanford CS classes are pass/fail for  MBA students and they'll be more  distractions whether that's starting a  company or starting a family post  graduation picture yourself as a coder  humor me with one more visualization  exercise come on  so picture yourself it's a Wednesday  afternoon you're sipping on your  favorite caffeinated beverage you're in  the office and your laptop spoiling way  taking care of your chores updating  sales statistics scraping down customer  data and then there's you doing what  really matters you're mentoring a high  potential new employee or you're  thinking through your company's next  strategic moves you're doing the things  you came to Stanford GSB to learn how to  do what an incredible vision  don't let the opportunity to make this a  reality slip by with confidence in your  own abilities take a class and start  coding  [Music]

